 below as we approach that number  and we're making this also a citizen  science project we just introduced the  global soil survey for sustainable turf  and we're hoping that hundreds or  thousands of people from around the  world will contribute samples from their  site  in order to help validate this for a  wider range of areas and for a wide  range of turf grasses  so those levels were  warm and cool  as far as we know they work warm and  cool and if  anybody  has any better information we're very  public about this we have a facebook  page we please contact us and let us  know that these numbers are wrong and we  eventually will actually share the data  and we'll share the software we're right  we're we're working with our  software and writing some scripts  i would hope people at cornell are also  using that it's it's awesome  so we're writing scripts to to do this  so  we will share that code also so people  can can see exactly what we've done so  we're we're trying to be very open about  this  and  we look at it as just an ongoing project  where we continually make these numbers  better and better i'm sure that because  of the way we're doing this eventually  this is going to be the standard  soil test recommendation  so if we consider those previous points  mathematically the minimum nutrient  requirement can be determined be it's  very simple i'm not going to explain it  right here it's in the handout and you  can see some more examples of that in  the handout and i link to other articles  i've written where i explained it  explicitly  but basically all we need to do  is we estimate the harvest or we measure  the harvest  so we know how much of an element we've  harvested we add that to the mlsn  guideline which makes sure that we're  keeping that amount in the soil and then  we subtract the amount that's on the  soil test if that's a positive number  that's how much of that element we need  to apply if that's a negative number  that means there's that much excess of  that element in the soil even accounting  for for we're assuming we're harvesting  all the clippings and we can estimate  the  the clipping yield based on on whatever  percent nitrogen there is in the leaves  so  we  we can do that that's all explained in  the handout if you're into turf and want  to see how we can do this so  that is  something that  we just recently did in the last couple  of years and we're still  working on this but we think that this  will be the way that people  choose to fertilize if they want to make  sure that they're applying the minimum  amount of fertilizer necessary to to  create still create high quality turf  grass conditions but not apply any  excess fertilizer  so there's more information at my  website i will post these slides and the  handout  and  the handout has links to all of the  websites i went to and links to lots  more additional reading so that will be  on my blog which you can access from  asianturfgrass.com tonight  thank you very much  this has been a production of cornell  university on the web at cornell.edu


